## Scope: this onboarding covers workspaces/boost/ only (2026-08-13)

rhdh-plugins is a 23-package Yarn monorepo, but this onboarding is
deliberately narrow: it covers only `workspaces/boost/` (the package
touching the AI Catalog Graduated Visibility Permissions work) and Jira
epic **RHIDP-15270** + its 6 child stories — not the other 22 workspace
packages or the wider RHIDP project's 166+ open issues. If work shifts to a
different workspace package or a different epic, treat this as a new
onboarding scope, not an extension of this one.

## When to recall (MUST)

You MUST call `recall` on your **first turn** when the task involves:
- TypeScript/React code in `workspaces/boost/` (Backstage plugins for AI
  Catalog / Graduated Visibility RBAC)
- Architecture, API design, or permission-model changes
- Debugging, troubleshooting, or root-cause analysis
- Anything the user has corrected you on before

Use the appropriate bank(s):
- **hindsight** — corrections, coding conventions, user preferences
- **rhdh-plugins-docs** — boost workspace specs/openspec docs (AI Catalog
  RBAC design, personas, permission model)
- **rhdh-plugins-issues** — Jira epic RHIDP-15270 and its child stories only

Call one or more banks depending on what the task touches. A single recall to
**hindsight** with a query summarizing the task intent is sufficient for most work.

## Before planning or implementing (MANDATORY GATE)

Before proposing any plan, writing any implementation, or switching to agent
mode to begin coding:

1. Recall `hindsight` with query: **"project methodology, workflow, conventions"**,
   passing `tags: ["rhdh-plugins"], tags_match: "any_strict"` explicitly.
   **Do not rely on the default or pass the literal string `"strict"`** —
   `tags_match` defaults to `"any"` (which *includes* untagged memories), and
   `"strict"` is not a valid value at all (real options: `any`, `all`,
   `any_strict`, `all_strict`, `exact`); an unrecognized value silently falls
   back to the unfiltered `"any"` default (see docs/FINDINGS.md in the
   engram repo, 2026-08-13 entry). This scopes results to rhdh-plugins-tagged
   facts only, avoiding kubernaut/dcm/koku/praxis/engram cross-project
   pollution.
2. Also fetch the 4 rhdh-plugins-scoped mental models
   (`rhdh-plugins-testing-methodology`, `rhdh-plugins-coding-conventions`,
   `rhdh-plugins-architecture-decisions`, `rhdh-plugins-workflow-preferences`)
   via `get_mental_model` and apply them as authoritative context alongside
   step 1's recall.
3. Apply the recalled methodology to your plan — TDD sequence, preflight
   checks, confidence gates.
4. If your plan does not follow the recalled methodology, **revise it before
   presenting**.

**Why the `tags` filter matters**: `cursor-memory` is a deliberately shared
bank across kubernaut/dcm/engram/koku/praxis/rhdh-plugins for universal
coding-hygiene lessons, but per-project mental models in it have
historically turned out to be language/project-specific despite carrying no
project tag (see docs/FINDINGS.md, 2026-07-27 incident) — an untagged recall
could surface Go/FedRAMP or Python/Django conventions as if they applied to
this TypeScript/React codebase. rhdh-plugins has its own isolated set
(above), tagged `["rhdh-plugins"]`, matched with `tags_match: "any_strict"`
so other projects' content can never enter them. **Known limitation**: only
newly retained facts carry a project tag going forward — an *untagged,
ad-hoc* `recall()` call (no `tags` param) can still surface old pre-fix
content from any project. Always pass
`tags: ["rhdh-plugins"], tags_match: "any_strict"` on cursor-memory recall
calls to get the isolation guarantee. Expect rhdh-plugins' own tagged
content to be sparse at first — it only grows as new retains happen
post-onboarding (2026-08-13).

This is a blocking gate. Do not skip it even if you believe you know the
methodology. Context summarization in long sessions can silently drop recalled
conventions — re-recall ensures they are current.

## Mid-session re-recall (long sessions)

In sessions longer than ~20 agent turns, or whenever a conversation summary
has been applied, **re-recall `hindsight`** with:
- "project methodology, workflow, conventions" — may have been lost to summarization
- Any topic-specific query relevant to the current phase

Rationale: empirical data shows 61% of corrections occur in the second half of
sessions. Re-recalling counteracts context loss from summarization and prevents
late-session mistakes.

## Code search: use `rhdh_plugins_code_search` tool

The MCP server **cocoindex-code** provides a tool named
**`rhdh_plugins_code_search`**. Call it with
`{"query": "your search terms", "limit": 10}`. Scoped to
`workspaces/boost/` only (see the onboarding-scope note above).

Use `rhdh_plugins_code_search` when:
- You need to find code by concept: `rhdh_plugins_code_search(query="how does graduated visibility permission checking work")`
- You need to locate an identifier: `rhdh_plugins_code_search(query="AiCatalogFilterBlueprint")`
- You want to understand a subsystem before modifying it
- You need to find callers or dependents of a function/component

Prefer `rhdh_plugins_code_search` over Grep/SemanticSearch when searching by
meaning. Use Grep only when you need exact regex matching.

## When to recall again (mid-session)

Call `recall` again if:
- The task shifts to a different subsystem, file area, or domain
- The user corrects you — check if this correction already exists in memory
- You've made 2+ failed attempts at the same problem
- A new entity (permission, blueprint, Jira story) enters the conversation
  that wasn't part of the original recall
- A code search returned results from an unfamiliar file — recall
  `hindsight` for conventions about that area

### Phase-based triggers (MUST recall when entering these phases)

| Phase | Bank or tool | Query focus |
|----|---|----|
| Implementation planning | hindsight | project methodology, TDD, preflight checks |
| Writing/designing tests | hindsight | test conventions, RTL patterns, assertion patterns |
| Designing permission model / APIs | rhdh-plugins-docs | AI Catalog RBAC design, permission contracts |
| PR/commit workflow | hindsight | commit conventions, PR structure, review expectations |
| Debugging/troubleshooting | rhdh-plugins-issues | epic/story status, known blockers |
| Exploring unfamiliar code | `rhdh_plugins_code_search` | semantic search for the feature/subsystem being investigated |
| Understanding blast radius | `rhdh_plugins_code_search` | what depends on the component/permission being changed |
| Refactoring / renaming | `rhdh_plugins_code_search` | all references and callers of the target symbol |

These are NOT optional. When you transition into one of these phases, recall
the indicated bank or call the indicated tool BEFORE writing code or proposing a plan.

## When to skip (OK)

- Trivial follow-ups ("yes", "do it", "commit")
- Pure file reads or terminal commands with no design decisions
- You already recalled this exact topic earlier in the session and nothing has changed

## How to use results

- **Mental models** (long synthesized documents): use directly as authoritative context
- **Individual facts**: reason over them to identify relevant patterns

## Data freshness

The `rhdh-plugins-docs` and `rhdh-plugins-issues` banks are continuously
updated via CocoIndex incremental sync (docs/code file-watching; Jira issues
polled every 5 minutes). Recalled doc/issue content is live — trust it
without re-reading source files unless the task requires verifying current state.

## TypeScript code: three-tier code guidance

Use the right tool for the right question:

- **Serena** (typescript-language-server-backed LSP) — precise type info,
  symbol references, symbol-level edits, diagnostics. Use for "what is the
  type of X?", "who calls this function/component?", "rename this symbol",
  "what errors exist?" Note (2026-08-13 spike): an unscoped whole-repo
  `find_symbol` on this 23-package monorepo can take ~2 minutes cold (first
  touch indexes every package); pass `relative_path` to scope the query and
  avoid this, or rely on the cache built up over the session.
- **`rhdh_plugins_code_search`** — semantic code search by meaning, scoped to
  `workspaces/boost/`. The tool is on the `cocoindex-code` MCP server. Call
  it with `{"query": "...", "limit": 10}`. Use for "where do we handle
  category-scoped permissions?", "how does SkillBundle RBAC filtering work?"
- **`rhdh_plugins_code_pattern_search`** — structural (by-example) code
  search, on the same `cocoindex-code` MCP server. Call it with
  `{"pattern": "export const \\NAME = (\\(A*\\)) =>", "language": "typescript"}`.
  Use for "find every function shaped like X" (a signature, not a
  name/concept) — purely syntactic, no type resolution or find-references;
  not a substitute for Serena.
- **hindsight** — past mistakes, conventions, behavioral patterns about code.
  Use for "how should I structure this test?", "what was corrected before?"

Prefer Serena for precise lookups/edits, `rhdh_plugins_code_search` for
semantic exploration, `rhdh_plugins_code_pattern_search` for structural
shape queries, and hindsight for behavioral context. Do not redundantly call
all of these for the same question.

## Do NOT retain

Never call `retain` during sessions. Memory extraction runs nightly.
